<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baghdadi Jews</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baghdadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were communities of Arabic-speaking Jews from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and neighbouring lands who settled in the trading ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> from the late 18th century. Their main centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and leading families such as the Sassoons built great commercial and industrial fortunes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, opium, shipping and banking.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdadi-jews.jpg" alt="Baghdadi Jews" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer, mid-19th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Sassoon_and_sons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jewish traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> since the time of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, when the caravan route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the spice trade tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>. Persian-speaking Jewish merchants, trading allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, Basra and Aleppo, were the first to reach the Indian heartland. Mughal sources first mention Jewish merchants from Baghdad trading with India in the 17th century, and some became courtiers: Shalom Cohen, who later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> community, was court jeweller to the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With the rise of British power, settlement shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and then principally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Leading families fleeing the persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawud-pasha">Dawud Pasha</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> strengthened the community there: a Jewish cemetery opened in 1812, a first synagogue in 1823, a second in 1856 and in 1884 a third, which was to be the largest in Asia. By the end of the 19th century more than 1,800 Baghdadi Jews lived in Calcutta. Their merchants dominated the export of Indian opium to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the Sassoon family eventually controlled 70 percent of that trade.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> began the end of this world. Refugees from the Japanese occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> swelled the Jewish population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to over 5,000, while much of the community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> was interned by the Japanese army. Fearing the violence of Partition and the policies of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Burma, their trade cut by the Communist victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and by later nationalisations, the Baghdadi Jews left for Israel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, almost all of them by the 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The communities were bound by religion, language and above all marriage: brides, and occasionally grooms, were sent from one community to another, and business alliances followed family ties. Most members were of Iraqi Jewish origin, but families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, joined and assimilated. Sir Manasseh Meyer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1846, schooled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and later a trader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> and Singapore, moved across Asia while staying inside this Judeo-Arabic speaking network. Their cuisine mixed Arab, Turkish, Persian and Indian influences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baghdadi Jews” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Land reclamation joined the islands, and Bombay became a hub of the cotton trade, especially during the American Civil War, and of textile mills, shipping and banking dominated by Parsi, Gujarati and <b>Baghdadi Jewish</b> merchants.</blockquote><a href="/state/bombay-presidency">Bombay Presidency</a></li></ul></section>
